  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThis approach introduces **hierarchical optimization** using multi-scale search across different solution topologies. Instead of optimizing within a fixed functional basis, we consider periodic patterns adapted from the problem structure and optimize continuous parameters for these patterns. This differs from previous work by:\n- Recognizing that optimal solutions may exhibit structured Fourier components aligned with the periodic [0,2] domain\n- Using mixed-integer programming to identify dominant frequencies\n- Parameterizing solutions using identified frequencies with cleaner gradient dynamics\n\nThe key limitations addressed are:\n- Local optimization easily gets trapped (addressed by multi-scale exploration)\n- Direct gradient calls may miss important structural patterns (replaced by basis-aligned optimization)\n- Existing methods struggle with high-dimensional repair (fixed basis functions simplify repair)\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent solutions using:\n- Primary variable: amplitude vector \u03b1 \u2208 \u211d^{n_bases}\n- Fourier basis matrices: F_k(x) = e^{2\u03c0i k\u00b7x / L} (for k=1 to n_bases)\n\nConstraints:\n\n0 \u2264 \u03b1_k \u2264 1  \u2200k\n\u222b h(x) dx = 1\nh(x) \u2208 {0,1}\n\nObjective:\n\nmin_\u03b1 (2/n) max_lag [\u222b h(x)(1-h(x+l))dx]\n\nImplementation:\n- Discover important frequencies via spectral initialization\n- Optimize amplitudes using gradient-based methods with projected constraints\n- Repair solutions using basis flexibility with clipping/normalization\n\nThe spectral initialization ensures global periodic structures, while parameter adjustment handles local variations efficiently.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n\na. Initialization:\n- Set domain: [0,2] discretized to n=560 points\n- Compute initial spectrum using Fast Fourier Transform for initial_h_values\n- Identify top n_bases = 30 frequencies with coherent components\n- Initialize amplitudes \u03b1 with known basis projection \u03bb \n\nb. Core optimization:\n- Define variables: \u03b8 = (\u03b8\u2081...\u03b8_{n_bases} ) where \u03b8_k = amplitude for k-th component\n- Objective function:\n    h(x) = sigmoid(\u2211_k \u03b8_k * cos(2\u03c0k x)) + noise\n    C_\u03b1 = max_lag correlation(h, 1-h)\n- Constraints:\n    \u2211_k |\u03b8_k|\u00b2 \u2264 C (periodicity threshold)\n    sum(\u03b8_k) \u2248 target (integral)\n\nc. Repair mechanism:\n- After each optimization step, apply:\n    h = clip(\u03b8, 0, 1)  (element-wise clipping)\n    normalize h to match integral\n\nd. Objective evaluation:\n    def evaluate_objective(\u03b8):\n        # Project \u03b8 to basis functions\n        h_values = np.zeros(n)\n        total_integral = 0\n        for k in range(len(\u03b8)):\n            comp = \u03b8[k] * np.cos(2\u03c0*k*x/L)\n            h_values += comp\n            total_integral += \u03b8[k]**2\n        # Clip and enforce constraints\n        h_values = np.clip(h_values, 0, 1)\n        h_values -= h_values.min()\n        h_values /= h_values.max()\n        h_values *= 0.5 * n / len(h_values) / max(1., h_values.max() or 1)\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n\nInitial parameters:\n- n_bases = 30 (decreases depending on time)\n- Temperature schedule: 100 to 1 (logarithmic cooling)\n- Mutation rate: 0.1 (basis relocation instead of amplitude jumps)\n\nAdaptive phases:\n- Phase I (coarse): temperature cooling from 100 to 10, 10^6 iterations\n- Phase II (fine): subgradient adjustment to match integrals\n- Temperature adaptivity: halve if no improvement in 10^4 iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n\nTime allocation:\n- 30% for basis detection\n- 40% for hierarchical optimization\n- 30% for verification\n\nValidation:\n- Early termination of unpromising branches (increase threshold if normalized correlation doesn't drop)\n- Repair check: skip if sum(\u03b8) continuously differs beyond 1e-3\n\nFallback strategy:\n- If temperature approaches minimum, switch to greedy hill climbing for 1000 steps\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n\nCheck final solution satisfies:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 \u2200i\n2. np.allclose(np.sum(h), n/2, rtol=1e-4)\n3. C_\u03b1 is well-defined and evaluated as (2.0/n) * max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n\nReturn format must include:\n- h_values (numpy array of length n)\n- C_5 evaluation at solution\n- n_points (exactly 560 or adjust for time)\n\nThe algorithm biases towards solutions with coherent spectral components, leveraging the problem's periodicity to overcome previous local optimization bottlenecks while maintaining exact gradient usage where possible.\n\nKey innovation: instead of generalized function spaces, we operate in a parameterized basis extracted from known minimizers, allowing more efficient exploration of function space under topological constraints.",
